Conditional Statements (if, if-else, else-if)

1. Conditional Statements

Conditional statements are used to execute code based on a particular condition.

Syntax:

if (condition) {
    // code executes if condition is true
}

2. if Statement

The if statement executes a block of code only when the given condition is true.
.
```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int age = 18;

    if (age >= 18) {
        printf("Eligible");
    }

    return 0;
}
```

### 3. if-else Statement
The `if-else` statement executes one block when the condition is true and another block when it is false.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int num = 7;

    if (num % 2 == 0) {
        printf("Even Number");
    } else {
        printf("Odd Number");
    }

    return 0;
}
```

### 4. else-if Ladder
The else-if ladder is used to check multiple conditions one by one.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int marks = 85;

    if (marks >= 90) {
        printf("Grade A");
    } else if (marks >= 75) {
        printf("Grade B");
    } else if (marks >= 60) {
        printf("Grade C");
    } else {
        printf("Grade D");
    }

    return 0;
}
```

### 5. Nested if
A nested if is an if statement inside another if statement.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int age = 20;
    int hasID = 1;

    if (age >= 18) {
        if (hasID == 1) {
            printf("Access Granted");
        }
    }

    return 0;
}
```

### 6. Important Operators
- `==` Equal to
- `!=` Not equal to
- `>` Greater than
- `<` Less than
- `>=` Greater than or equal to
- `<=` Less than or equal to
- `&&` Logical AND
- `||` Logical OR
- `!` Logical NOT

### 7. Key Points
- Conditions return either true (1) or false (0).
- Curly braces `{}` define a code block.
- `else` cannot exist without an `if`.
- Conditions are checked from top to bottom in an else-if ladder.
- Once a condition is true, its block executes and the remaining ladder is skipped.
